
  <sn><md>## How PDF.js does it

When you pass a URL, PDF.js doesn't wait for the whole file. It uses `fetch()` and reads `response.body` as a stream, one chunk at a time. After each chunk it adds the chunk's byte length to a `loaded` counter, reads `total` from the `Content-Length` header, and calls `onProgress({ loaded, total })`.

That's why the callback checks `if (total)`: if the server doesn't send `Content-Length`, `total` is unknown and a percentage can't be calculated.

## A reusable fetch with progress

```js
async function fetchWithProgress(url, { onProgress, signal, ...options } = {}) {
  const response = await fetch(url, { signal, ...options });
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);

  const total = Number(response.headers.get("Content-Length")) || 0;
  const reader = response.body.getReader();
  const chunks = [];
  let loaded = 0;

  while (true) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;

    chunks.push(value);
    loaded += value.length;
    onProgress?.({ loaded, total });
  }

  // merge chunks into one Uint8Array
  const data = new Uint8Array(loaded);
  let offset = 0;
  for (const chunk of chunks) {
    data.set(chunk, offset);
    offset += chunk.length;
  }

  return data; // Uint8Array
}
```

### Usage

```js
const controller = new AbortController();

const data = await fetchWithProgress("https://example.com/file.pdf", {
  signal: controller.signal,
  onProgress: ({ loaded, total }) =&gt; {
    if (total) setProgress((loaded / total) * 100);
    else console.log(`${loaded} bytes loaded`);
  },
});

// pass the bytes straight to PDF.js
const pdf = await pdfjsLib.getDocument({ data }).promise;

// cancel anytime: controller.abort();
```

Passing `{ data }` means PDF.js doesn't fetch again, so you control the progress UI yourself.

## Things to watch

- **CORS:** for cross-origin files, the server must send `Access-Control-Expose-Headers: Content-Length`, or `total` will be `0`.
- **Compression:** if the response is gzip/brotli encoded, `Content-Length` is the compressed size but `loaded` counts decompressed bytes, so the percentage can exceed 100. Clamp it with `Math.min(100, ...)`.
- **Same-origin PDFs:** these usually work fine, since static servers normally send `Content-Length` for `.pdf` files.

